unit(4,"Simple present and simple past","choose between a present habit and a completed past event and form a past negative correctly",
 "parts of speech and the tense overview","transitivity, future forms and later narrative tense contrasts",
 "The simple present commonly expresses habits or general states; the simple past commonly presents a completed event in a past context. For a third-person singular present subject, ordinary verbs usually add -s or -es. Past negatives and questions with did use the base verb: did not go, not did not went. Be has its own forms. Time words guide interpretation but do not replace reading the meaning. Compare the present habit with the completed past event before choosing a form.",
 "Original contrast: Bih {thirdPerson('buy')} materials on market days. Yesterday she {past('buy')} an item. She did not buy it the day before. The negative uses did to carry the past; bought must not be added after did. In a habit report, yesterday cannot simply be attached to the present form without changing the intended construction.",
 "Identify the time frame and whether the sentence is affirmative, negative or a question. Check subject agreement in the present and irregular forms in the past. Preserve the meaning when correcting; do not add two past markers around did.",[
 F("present habit","a repeated activity described with the simple present","Bih buys supplies on market days.","Every habitual sentence must be a past event.","The simple present can report a repeated activity."),F("completed past event","an action presented as finished in its past context","Yesterday Bih {past('buy')} an item.","Yesterday Bih buy the item is the standard affirmative model.","Use the simple past for the completed event in this model."),F("past negative","did not followed by the base verb for an ordinary verb","Bih did not buy the item.","Bih did not bought the item is the standard model.","Did carries the past; the following verb stays in the base form."),F("present agreement","the third-person singular form with an appropriate subject","Ayuk {thirdPerson('choose')} the book in the stated habit.","He choose is always the neutral school agreement pattern.","The singular third-person subject requires the appropriate present form.")],pastq,prereq=[3],extra=choose(4,[("Correct the past negative.","Bih did not go.",["Bih did not went.","Bih do not went.","Bih not went."],["After did use go.","The auxiliary and lexical form are wrong for this past negative.","An ordinary past negative needs did not in this model."],"Did not takes the base go."),
 ("Correct the question about a past purchase.","Did Ayuk buy it?",["Did Ayuk bought it?","Does Ayuk bought it?","Did Ayuk buys it?"],["Use buy after did.","This mixes present auxiliary with a past form.","Do not add third-person -s after did."],"Did carries the past in the question."),
 ("Choose the ordinary present habit with she.","She reads after chores.",["She read every evening, in the stated present habit.","She reading after chores.","She do reads after chores."],["The explicit present-habit context needs third-person agreement.","The phrase lacks a finite verb.","After do the ordinary form would be base; this is not the requested neutral habit pattern."],"She takes reads in this model."),
 ("Choose the finished event in the stated past context.","They arrived yesterday.",["They arrives yesterday.","They arriving yesterday.","They did arrived yesterday."],["That present singular form does not fit.","A finite verb is missing.","Did would take arrive, not arrived."],"Arrived is a finite simple-past form.")],key="repair-pattern"))

unit(11,"Transitive and intransitive verb uses","identify a direct object and distinguish transitive from intransitive uses in context",
 "word functions and finite clause patterns","adverbials, clause structure and accurate sentence construction",
 "A transitive use takes a direct object: Bih opened the box. An intransitive use has no direct object: the box opened. A verb can allow both patterns, so classify the use in the sentence rather than attaching one permanent label to every spelling. A place or time phrase is not automatically a direct object. Linking complements also need a different analysis: happy in she seems happy describes the subject rather than naming an object acted on.",
 "Original pairs: Ayuk writes a report; Ayuk writes after supper. The report is a direct object in the first use. After supper gives time in the second. Bih opened the door; the door opened. The change affects the pattern even though the verb is the same. Do not insert an object into arrive merely because another verb accepts one.",
 "Find the verb use, then ask whether a noun phrase functions as its direct object. Distinguish object from an adverbial phrase or subject complement. Test the complete clause and its meaning instead of counting how many words follow the verb.",[
 F("transitive use","a verb use taking a direct object","Bih opened the box; consider the verb’s relation to the box.","Any word after a verb must be its direct object.","A direct object has a grammatical role, not merely a position after a verb."),F("intransitive use","a verb use without a direct object","The box opened; no direct object follows opened.","A verb with no direct object can never form a complete clause.","An intransitive use can be complete without a direct object."),F("adverbial phrase","a phrase giving circumstances such as time or place","Ayuk writes after supper; after supper gives time.","After supper is necessarily the direct object of writes.","The phrase states when, rather than what is written."),F("subject complement","an expression describing or identifying the subject after a linking verb","Bih seems happy; happy describes Bih.","Happy is always a direct object receiving an action in seems happy.","The linking pattern relates the description to the subject.")],
 choose(11,[("In Ayuk reads a story, identify the direct object.","a story",["Ayuk","reads","There is no direct object."],["Ayuk is the subject.","Reads is the verb.","A story is the noun phrase read."],"The story is what Ayuk reads."),
 ("In Bih arrived after lunch, classify arrived in this sentence.","intransitive use",["transitive use with after lunch as object","an adjective modifying lunch","a noun naming Bih"],["After lunch states time, not a direct object.","Arrived is the verb.","It is not the naming expression."],"No direct object is present."),
 ("Which sentence uses opened transitively?","Bih opened the door.",["The door opened.","The door opened slowly.","The door opened yesterday."],["The door is the subject here.","Slowly adds manner, not a direct object.","Yesterday adds time, not a direct object."],"The door is the direct object of Bih’s action in the correct sentence."),
 ("In Bih seems tired, what is tired?","a subject complement",["a direct object","a time adverbial","the subject"],["It describes Bih through a linking verb.","It does not state when.","Bih is the subject."],"The adjective describes the subject.")]),prereq=[3,4])

unit(12,"Adverbs of frequency, place and manner","identify what an adverb contributes and choose a suitable position in a clear model sentence",
 "word classes, verb uses and adverbial phrases","precise description and narrative detail",
 "Frequency tells how often, place tells where and manner tells how an action happens. Often, outside and carefully illustrate these functions. A common frequency position is before an ordinary main verb but after be: Bih often reads; she is often early. Manner and place positions depend on the clause and emphasis. Some standard alternatives are possible, so a preferred model is not the only grammatical order. Not every -ly word is an adverb, and not every adverb ends in -ly.",
 "Original model: Ayuk often studies outside. He reads carefully. Often describes frequency, outside location and carefully manner. Compare a careful reader with reads carefully: the adjective describes the noun, while the adverb modifies the action. Never already has negative force in the neutral standard pattern used here.",
 "Ask which question the expression answers: how often, where or how. Check its relation to the verb and the rest of the sentence. Avoid unnecessary double negatives and preserve meaning when moving an adverb. Accept a standard alternative order when context supports it.",[
 F("frequency","how often an event happens","Ayuk often reads after supper.","Often tells the colour of the book.","Often describes recurrence, not colour."),F("place","where an event occurs","Ayuk studies outside.","Outside here specifies how often he studies.","It locates the activity rather than giving its frequency."),F("manner","how an action is performed","Bih reads carefully.","Carefully must be an adjective describing Bih’s height.","It modifies how she reads."),F("adjective/adverb contrast","choosing a describing form according to what it modifies","A careful reader reads carefully.","A carefully reader is the neutral adjective pattern.","Use careful to modify reader and carefully to modify reads.")],
 choose(12,[("Choose the manner form: Bih checks the work ___.","carefully",["careful","carefulness","care"],["Careful is the adjective.","Carefulness is a noun.","Care is not the manner adverb required."],"The adverb modifies checks."),
 ("Which word tells place in Ayuk often studies outside?","outside",["often","studies","Ayuk"],["Often gives frequency.","Studies is the verb.","Ayuk is the subject."],"Outside locates the studying."),
 ("Choose the common neutral position with be.","Bih is often early.",["Bih often early.","Bih is oftenly early.","Bih is early oftenly."],["The finite verb is missing.","Oftenly is not the standard adverb.","Oftenly is not the standard adverb."],"Often commonly follows be."),
 ("Choose the standard neutral negative-frequency model.","Ayuk never misses the club.",["Ayuk does not never miss the club.","Ayuk never miss the club, as a present habit.","Ayuk is never misses the club."],["Never already supplies the negative meaning in this pattern.","The singular subject needs misses.","Do not add be before this simple-present verb."],"Never and subject agreement fit the model.")]),prereq=[11])

unit(15,"Future reference with will and other forms","form will plus a base verb and choose a future expression suited to its context",
 "the simple present/past contrast and auxiliary patterns","conditional sentences about possible future situations",
 "Will + base verb can express a prediction, offer or decision, depending on context. Do not add -s or to to the lexical verb after will. Future reference also uses other patterns: be going to for a prior intention or evidence-based prediction, present progressive for an arrangement and simple present for a timetable. These uses overlap in real language. An exercise should specify its intended context rather than mark every alternative future form wrong.",
 "Original dialogue: Bih notices the books on the floor and says, I will help. Ayuk already has a plan: I am going to organise the shelf. Their agreed meeting is tomorrow, while the printed timetable gives a fixed opening time. Each choice presents the future differently; will does not automatically mean a guaranteed fact.",
 "Identify prediction, immediate offer, prior plan, arrangement or timetable. Check the auxiliary and base form. A question with will places it before the subject. Keep the same meaning when correcting the form rather than inventing a new intention.",[
 F("will + base verb","a future-reference pattern without an added -s or to","Bih will help with the books.","Bih will helps is the standard pattern.","The lexical verb stays in its base form after will."),F("immediate offer","a willingness to act expressed in response to the situation","Seeing a difficulty, Bih says I will help.","An immediate offer must always describe a completed past event.","The speaker offers a response at that point."),F("prior intention","a plan already formed before the current exchange","Ayuk says he is going to organise the shelf he planned to sort.","Every future plan can only be expressed with one form in every context.","Going to is a useful model for the prior intention, without excluding all alternatives."),F("timetable reference","future reference using a scheduled event","The printed notice says the room opens tomorrow morning.","A present form can never refer to a scheduled future event.","Simple present can describe timetable events.")],
 choose(15,[("Complete: Bih will ___ the list.","write",["writes","to write","writing"],["Do not add third-person -s after will.","Will takes a base verb without to.","An -ing form does not complete this simple will pattern."],"Will is followed by the base write."),
 ("Choose the correct question.","Will Ayuk bring the book?",["Will Ayuk brings the book?","Does Ayuk will bring the book?","Will bring Ayuk the book?"],["Use bring without -s.","Do not combine does and will in this question.","Place the subject after will."],"Will precedes the subject and base verb."),
 ("Choose the complete going-to plan.","Bih is going to read.",["Bih going to read.","Bih is going read.","Bih is go to read."],["The finite form of be is missing.","The infinitive marker to is missing in this pattern.","Use going after be here."],"The pattern is be going to + base verb."),
 ("Choose a correctly formed negative with will.","Ayuk will not leave yet.",["Ayuk will not left yet.","Ayuk does not will leave yet.","Ayuk will not leaves yet."],["Use base leave after will.","Will itself forms this negative without does.","Do not add -s after will."],"Will not takes the base verb.")]),prereq=[4])

unit(17,"Conditional sentences","match a common conditional pattern to its meaning and correct a malformed clause",
 "past, present and future-reference forms","reasoned argument and later imagined or past situations",
 "A conditional links a condition and a result. Common zero-condition models use present forms for general patterns. A first-condition model uses if + present and will + base for a possible future. A second-condition model commonly uses if + past and would + base for an imagined present or future. A third-condition model uses if + past perfect and would have + participle for an unreal past. These are useful patterns, not a claim that every possible conditional must fit one rigid template.",
 "Original models: If the room is available, the club meets there as a general rule. If it is available tomorrow, we will meet there. If we had another room, we would share the work. If we had booked earlier, we would have obtained the room. The last situation is imagined about the past; it does not report that the earlier booking actually happened.",
 "Choose the intended time and degree of reality before the form. In the ordinary first-condition model, do not insert will into the if-clause simply because the time is future. Other modal uses exist, so a special context can justify another construction. Clause order can change without reversing the condition and result.",[
 F("zero-condition model","a condition/result pattern describing a general relationship","If the room is available, the club meets there under its general rule.","Every conditional necessarily concerns one future event.","A zero-condition model can describe a general pattern."),F("first-condition model","a common if-present/will-base pattern for a possible future","If the room is free tomorrow, we will meet there.","The ordinary first-condition model always requires will in both clauses.","Use present in the if-clause and will plus base in the result here."),F("second-condition model","an if-past/would-base pattern for an imagined present or future","If we had another room now, we would use it.","The past form here proves the imagined room existed yesterday.","The past form can mark hypothetical distance rather than past time."),F("third-condition model","an if-past-perfect/would-have-participle pattern for an unreal past","If we had booked earlier, we would have obtained the room.","The third-condition model reports that the earlier condition actually happened.","The model imagines a past alternative, rather than asserting its occurrence.")],
 choose(17,[("Choose the ordinary possible-future model.","If Bih arrives early, she will help.",["If Bih will arrives early, she will help.","If Bih arrive early, she will helped.","If Bih arrived yesterday, she will helped yesterday."],["The ordinary if-clause uses present, with correct agreement.","Arrives and help are required in this pattern.","This mixes incompatible time and verb patterns."],"If-present links to will plus base."),
 ("Choose the imagined present/future model.","If Ayuk had more time, he would read.",["If Ayuk had more time, he would reads.","If Ayuk has more time, he would to read.","If Ayuk had more time, he will reading."],["Use base read after would.","Would does not take to in this pattern.","Will reading does not form the requested model."],"If-past plus would-base fits the stated imagined situation."),
 ("Choose the unreal-past model.","If Bih had left earlier, she would have arrived on time.",["If Bih had leave earlier, she would have arrived on time.","If Bih had left earlier, she would have arrive on time.","If Bih had left earlier, she would has arrived on time."],["Had takes the participle left.","Have takes the participle arrived.","Would is followed by base have."],"Both perfect patterns need the appropriate participles."),
 ("Which reformulation preserves the conditional relationship?","We will meet if the room is free.",["The room is free if we will meet, with the same asserted condition.","We will meet although the room is free, with identical meaning.","The room was free because we met, with identical meaning."],["That changes the condition’s direction and form.","Although expresses concession, not the same condition.","That invents past time and a causal relationship."],"Moving the if-clause does not reverse which event is the condition.")]),prereq=[4,15])

unit(18,"Prepositions of time, place, purpose and direction","choose a preposition or related purpose construction from the meaning of the whole phrase",
 "adverbials and clause relationships","accurate descriptions, reports and movement narratives",
 "Prepositions express relations such as time, location or movement. In a simple school model, at can mark a clock time, on a day/date and in a longer period. In and on can locate something inside a space or on a surface; to can mark destination and into movement inside. For + noun can express purpose or intended use; to + base verb is an infinitive purpose construction, not the same grammatical pattern. Fixed combinations and context create exceptions, so do not apply a single word rule everywhere.",
 "Original model: The club meets on Monday in the afternoon. Bih waits at the entrance, walks into the room and puts a notebook on the table. A shelf is for books; members gather to read. The direction into differs from a position already in the room. Read the complete relation before choosing a small word.",
 "Ask whether the phrase gives time, position, movement or purpose. Check what follows: for a noun, to a base verb in the purpose model. Do not confuse the prepositional use of to before a destination with every infinitive use of to.",[
 F("time relation","a phrase locating an event in a temporal frame","The club meets on Monday.","On Monday gives the direction of movement.","The named day locates time rather than destination."),F("position","a location without asserting movement into it","The notebook is on the table.","On the table always means moving inside the table.","It states where the notebook is in this model."),F("direction","a relation showing movement towards or into a place","Bih walks into the room.","Into can only describe a stationary position with no movement.","Into expresses the movement inside here."),F("purpose construction","wording expressing intended use or aim","A shelf is for books; members meet to read.","For books and to read must have identical grammatical structure.","A noun phrase after for differs from an infinitive after to.")],
 choose(18,[("Complete the day phrase: We meet ___ Monday.","on",["into","through","under"],["Into expresses a different relation.","Through is not the day preposition in this model.","Under does not mark the day here."],"Use on for the named day in this model."),
 ("Choose the movement inside.","Bih walks into the room.",["Bih walks under Monday.","Bih walks for read.","Bih walks in tomorrow the room."],["This mixes place and time without sense.","For read is not the required movement pattern.","The phrase is malformed and does not give the requested model."],"Into marks movement inside."),
 ("Complete the purpose: We meet ___ discuss books.","to",["for","on","at"],["For would need another construction, such as for discussion.","On does not form this infinitive purpose.","At does not form this infinitive purpose."],"To plus base discuss expresses the purpose."),
 ("Complete the surface position: The book is ___ the table.","on",["into","towards","throughout"],["Into suggests movement inside, not the stated surface position.","Towards gives direction.","Throughout does not express this ordinary surface relation."],"On expresses the surface position.")]),prereq=[12,17])

unit(21,"Sentence types and communicative purpose","distinguish declarative, interrogative, imperative and exclamative forms without relying on punctuation alone",
 "verb and clause patterns in statements and questions","argumentative writing and sentence-structure analysis",
 "A declarative normally states information; an interrogative normally asks a question; an imperative can give an instruction or request; an exclamative expresses a strong reaction through its construction. Form and communicative purpose can differ: Could you close the door? is interrogative in form but can function as a polite request. An exclamation mark alone does not turn every sentence into an exclamative structure. Read the grammar and situation, not merely the final sign.",
 "Original models: Bih has the book. Has Bih brought it? Bring the book, please. What a useful book it is! The imperative normally omits the subject you. A question can also request an action, so identify whether an exercise asks for grammatical form or communicative purpose.",
 "Find the finite verb, subject pattern and any imperative or exclamative construction. Label form and function separately when needed. Use punctuation to support the construction instead of letting it decide everything by itself.",[
 F("declarative","a statement-form clause in the model","Bih has the book.","Every declarative must ask a direct question.","This form states information."),F("interrogative","a question-form clause in the model","Has Bih brought the book?","A polite request can never use interrogative form.","A question form can function as a request in context."),F("imperative","a directive form normally without an expressed you subject","Bring the book, please.","An imperative always needs you written before the base verb.","The directive model normally leaves you unexpressed."),F("exclamative","a construction expressing a strong reaction, such as what a or how","What a useful book it is!","Any sentence becomes an exclamative structure solely by adding an exclamation mark.","The construction, not just the mark, matters.")],
 choose(21,[("Identify the form of Could you pass the book?","interrogative",["declarative","imperative form","noun phrase only"],["It has question structure.","Its request function does not change its interrogative form.","It is a full question clause."],"Separate grammatical question form from request function."),
 ("Choose the imperative form.","Read the notice, please.",["Did you read the notice?","You read the notice yesterday.","What a clear notice it is!"],["This is interrogative.","This is declarative in the given past context.","This is exclamative."],"The base verb directs an action without an expressed subject."),
 ("Choose the exclamative construction.","What a clear explanation that is!",["Is the explanation clear?","The explanation is clear.","Explain clearly, please."],["This asks a question.","This is a declarative statement.","This gives an instruction."],"What a introduces the exclamative model."),
 ("Identify the form of Bih finished the task.","declarative",["interrogative","imperative","exclamative construction"],["There is no question structure here.","It reports an event instead of directing Bih.","It lacks the exclamative construction studied."],"The clause presents a statement.")]),prereq=[3,11])

unit(23,"Simple, compound, complex and compound-complex sentences","classify sentence structure by independent and dependent clauses and repair a comma splice",
 "sentence types, conjunctions and finite clause patterns","organised narratives and varied formal writing",
 "A simple sentence has one independent clause; it can still have a compound subject or verb. A compound sentence has at least two independent clauses. A complex sentence has an independent clause and at least one dependent clause. A compound-complex sentence combines at least two independent clauses with a dependent clause. Length and the mere presence of and do not determine the type. A comma alone does not normally join independent clauses in formal writing; use an appropriate conjunction or punctuation.",
 "Original set: Bih and Ayuk read quietly: simple. Bih reads, and Ayuk writes: compound. Bih reads because the room is quiet: complex. Bih reads because the room is quiet, and Ayuk writes: compound-complex. Count clauses by their structure, not names or verbs in a list.",
 "Mark the boundaries, identify clauses that can stand independently and note dependent markers. Check whether and joins words, phrases or clauses. When repairing a comma splice, preserve the intended relationship rather than inserting any connector at random.",[
 F("simple sentence","a sentence with one independent clause","Bih and Ayuk read quietly.","Every sentence containing and is compound.","And can join subjects within a single clause."),F("compound sentence","a sentence with at least two independent clauses and no dependent clause in this classification","Bih reads, and Ayuk writes.","A compound sentence must contain only one independent clause.","Both clauses can stand independently."),F("complex sentence","a sentence combining an independent clause with a dependent clause","Bih reads because the room is quiet.","A because-clause alone is always a complete independent sentence.","The dependent clause needs its main clause in this model."),F("compound-complex sentence","a sentence combining multiple independent clauses with a dependent clause","Bih reads because it is quiet, and Ayuk writes.","Sentence length alone proves that a sentence is compound-complex.","The number and dependence of clauses decide the classification.")],
 choose(23,[("Classify Bih and Ayuk write reports.","simple",["compound","complex","compound-complex"],["And joins subjects, not independent clauses.","There is no dependent clause.","There are not multiple independent clauses plus a dependent one."],"There is one independent clause."),
 ("Classify Bih writes because the club needs a report.","complex",["simple","compound","compound-complex"],["Because introduces a dependent clause.","The because-clause is not independent.","There are not two independent clauses."],"An independent and a dependent clause form the complex model."),
 ("Classify Bih writes, and Ayuk checks the list.","compound",["simple","complex","compound-complex"],["There are two independent clauses.","No dependent clause is present.","No dependent clause is present."],"And coordinates independent clauses."),
 ("Repair the comma splice without changing the two events.","Bih writes the report, and Ayuk checks it.",["Bih writes the report, Ayuk checks it.","Because Bih writes the report.","Bih writing the report and Ayuk checking it."],["The comma alone still joins independent clauses.","This leaves a dependent fragment and omits the other event.","The finite clauses have become incomplete phrases."],"The coordinator joins the independent clauses.")]),prereq=[17,21])

unit(29,"Simple past and past perfect","show the order of past events with simple past and had plus a participle",
 "simple past and the overview of perfect forms","clear chronology in narratives and reports",
 "The simple past can tell a completed past sequence. The past perfect uses had + past participle to look back from a later past point. It helps show an earlier event or situation relevant at that point. Do not use had plus the simple-past form when the participle differs. Past perfect is not required for every earlier event if order is already clear; use it to make the intended perspective clear rather than marking ordinary chronological narration wrong.",
 "Original model: When Ayuk arrived, Bih had {participle('write')} the note. Writing came before the later arrival. Compare Bih {past('write')} the note and then Ayuk arrived, which directly narrates the sequence. Had written supplies the look-back relationship; had wrote does not give the standard form.",
 "Choose the later past reference point, identify the earlier event and select a participle. Keep the same event order when rewriting. A sequence word such as before can already clarify order, so context should determine whether past perfect is needed or simply useful.",[
 F("simple-past sequence","completed events narrated in their stated order","Bih wrote the note and then Ayuk arrived.","A past narrative cannot use more than one simple-past verb.","Simple past can narrate a chronological sequence."),F("past perfect","had plus a past participle looking back from a past point","Bih had {participle('write')} the note before the later arrival.","Had wrote is the standard perfect form of write.","Had takes the participle written."),F("later reference point","the past event from which an earlier situation is viewed","Ayuk’s arrival is the later point in when he arrived, Bih had written.","The later reference point must always occur in the future.","Both events can be past; the reference point is later within that past."),F("chronological signal","wording clarifying the order of events","Then and before can help show the sequence.","Every earlier action must use past perfect even when the order is already clear.","Context and intended perspective determine the tense choice.")],
 mcq("e4-29-participle",2,"Complete the look-back: Before the later meeting, Bih had ___ the item. Base verb: {v}.","{participle(v)}",[("{past(v)}","past"),("{v}","base"),("{ing(v)}","ing")],["The pattern is had + past participle.","The participle of {v} is {answer}."],vars={"v":{"pick":["take","choose","write","see"]}},misc=[("past","That is the simple-past form, not the participle needed after had."),("base","Had requires the participle here, not the base form."),("ing","Had plus -ing alone does not form this past perfect.")],sources=SRC),prereq=[4,25])

# Vocabulary and phrase-building.
unit(16,"Context clues and collocation","infer a supported word meaning from context and choose a natural word combination",
 "word classes and sentence meaning","careful reading of trade passages and precise writing",
 "A context clue is evidence from surrounding language. A definition, contrast, example or consequence can help explain an unfamiliar word. The clue supports a meaning; it does not justify any guess that sounds interesting. A collocation is a conventional word combination, such as make a decision or heavy rain. A synonym may fit a definition yet sound unnatural with a particular partner. Check grammar and meaning together, and use a dictionary when the context is insufficient.",
 "Original example: The path was slippery, so Bih moved carefully to avoid falling. The consequence supports a surface on which slipping is easy. A club can make a decision and do homework, but exchanging make and do mechanically creates unnatural combinations. Heavy rain is ordinary wording; strong rain is not the neutral model taught here.",
 "Locate the exact clue and explain how it supports your interpretation. Separate a contextual inference from a fact not given. When choosing a collocation, consider the whole phrase rather than the isolated meaning of one word.",[
 F("definition clue","surrounding wording that explains the unfamiliar expression","The passage explains a kiosk as a small stall.","A definition clue requires no explanatory wording.","The nearby explanation supplies the meaning."),F("contrast clue","opposed wording that helps reveal a meaning","Unlike the dry floor, the wet path was slippery.","A contrast always means the words are synonyms.","Opposition can distinguish the relevant properties."),F("collocation","a conventional combination of words","The group makes a decision.","Any synonym can replace any word in a collocation naturally.","Conventional combinations constrain the choice."),F("supported inference","a conclusion linked to clues actually provided","Moving carefully to avoid falling supports slippery as easy to slip on.","A guess with no textual clue is automatically proven.","An inference must connect to evidence in the passage.")],
 choose(16,[("Complete the neutral collocation: ___ a decision.","make",["do","performing","strong"],["Do is not the ordinary collocation here.","This form does not fit the verb slot.","Strong does not fill the verb slot."],"Make a decision is the model combination."),
 ("Complete the neutral collocation: ___ homework.","do",["make","heavy","decision"],["Make is not the neutral model for this activity.","Heavy is not the required verb.","Decision is a noun, not the required verb."],"Do homework is the model combination."),
 ("The path was slippery, so Bih moved carefully to avoid falling. Which meaning is best supported?","easy to slip on",["guaranteed to be dry","full of books","closed forever"],["The fall risk does not prove dryness.","No books are mentioned.","No permanent closure is stated."],"The fall risk supports the surface meaning."),
 ("Choose the neutral combination.","heavy rain",["do rain","make rain, meaning the natural weather intensity","homework rain"],["Do does not express rainfall intensity here.","Make rain changes the meaning to causing rain.","Homework does not form this phrase."],"Heavy naturally describes the rain’s intensity in this model.")]),prereq=[3,12])

unit(24,"Homonyms, homophones and homographs","distinguish sound, spelling and meaning while recognising that terminology can overlap",
 "context clues, word classes and written sound symbols","editing narrative vocabulary and avoiding wrong-word spellings",
 "Homophones share pronunciation while differing in meaning; their spellings may differ, as with hear and here. Homographs share spelling but differ in meaning and may differ in pronunciation, as with lead a group and lead the metal. Homonym has both a broad dictionary use and a narrower school use for words sharing spelling and pronunciation with different meanings. This lesson states the narrower use when needed. Categories can overlap, so do not force one exclusive label where the question has not specified its criterion.",
 "Original set: Hear the speaker here. A bat can be an animal or sports equipment in the narrow homonym model. Lead a group differs from lead the metal in the pronunciation model. Spell-check alone may not detect a word that is spelled correctly but has the wrong meaning for the sentence.",
 "Ask whether the exercise compares sound, spelling or both. Read the context to identify meaning. The screen can compare written examples and transcriptions, but actual pronunciation needs a teacher or recording. Confirm the school’s preferred homonym terminology.",[
 F("homophone relation","shared pronunciation with different meanings","Hear and here illustrate a sound-based relation.","Homophones must have identical meanings.","The shared sound does not make their meanings identical."),F("homograph relation","shared spelling with different meanings","Lead a group and lead the metal share their written form.","Homographs must always have the same pronunciation.","The relation is based on spelling; pronunciation can differ."),F("narrow homonym use","shared spelling and pronunciation with different meanings in the specified school model","Bat the animal and bat the sports item illustrate the narrow model.","The word homonym has only one definition in every source.","Dictionaries and courses can use broad or narrow definitions; state the convention."),F("contextual word choice","selecting the meaning and spelling required by the sentence","Hear the speaker here uses each word in its proper role.","A correct spelling guarantees the right word for the context.","A word can be spelled correctly but express the wrong meaning.")],
 choose(24,[("Complete the request to listen: ___ the speaker.","Hear",["Here","Hair","Hare"],["Here indicates place.","Hair names a different thing.","Hare names an animal."],"Hear expresses listening to sound."),
 ("Which criterion identifies homographs?","shared spelling",["identical meanings","different spelling in every case","same sentence length"],["The meanings differ.","Homographs share spelling.","Sentence length is irrelevant."],"Graph concerns the written form."),
 ("Under the stated narrow model, which pair is a homonym example?","bat the animal / bat the sports item",["hear / here, as different spellings","large / small","read / reader, as a word-family pair"],["This pair shares sound but not spelling.","These are contrasting meanings with different forms.","A suffix relation is different from the stated homonym relation."],"The narrow example shares spelling and pronunciation with different meanings."),
 ("Choose the place word: Put the bag ___.","here",["hear","hair","hare"],["Hear concerns sound.","Hair does not fill the place role.","Hare does not fill the place role."],"Here locates the bag.")]),prereq=[16])

unit(31,"Prefixes, suffixes, synonyms and antonyms","use a word’s structure and context to choose a related form or meaning",
 "word classes, contextual clues and homonym distinctions","editing reports and building precise vocabulary",
 "A prefix attaches before a base, while a suffix attaches after it. Either can affect meaning, and suffixes can change word class. Unhappy uses a negative prefix; helpful uses a suffix to form an adjective. Not every visible letter sequence is a productive affix. Synonyms have related meanings in context, while antonyms oppose meanings on a relevant scale. A word may have several senses, so neither a prefix nor a dictionary synonym permits every possible substitution.",
 "Original model: help, helpful and unhelpful belong to a word family. A calm room may be described as peaceful; an open door contrasts with a closed door. Replacing peaceful with an unrelated look-alike word would not preserve the description. A change of suffix must still fit the required grammatical position.",
 "Find the base, identify the added part and check the resulting word in its sentence. For synonyms and antonyms, preserve the relevant sense and grammatical construction. Verify unfamiliar forms rather than adding un- or -ness to every word mechanically.",[
 F("prefix","an element added before a base","Un- precedes happy in unhappy.","A prefix always follows its base.","Its position is before the base."),F("suffix","an element added after a base","-ful follows help in helpful.","Every suffix leaves word class unchanged.","Suffixes can alter class as well as meaning."),F("synonym in context","a word with a related meaning in the stated use","Calm and peaceful can describe the same quiet room.","A synonym must fit every possible sense of a word.","Related meanings remain context-dependent."),F("antonym in context","a word expressing an opposed meaning on the relevant aspect","Open and closed contrast the door’s state.","Antonyms are defined only by similar spelling.","The opposition is semantic, not merely visual.")],
 choose(31,[("Which part is a prefix in unhappy?","un-",["-ness","-ful","happy"],["That suffix is not present.","That suffix is not present.","Happy is the base."],"Un- is added before the base."),
 ("Choose the adjective: a ___ response.","helpful",["helpfully","helpfulness","helpingness"],["Helpfully is the adverb.","Helpfulness is a noun.","This invented form is not the standard adjective."],"Helpful modifies response."),
 ("Choose a contextual synonym for calm in a calm room.","peaceful",["noisy","narrow","expensive"],["Noisy opposes the quiet atmosphere.","Narrow concerns width.","Expensive concerns cost."],"Peaceful preserves the intended atmosphere."),
 ("Choose the opposite of open for the stated door position.","closed",["wide","welcome","opening"],["Wide is not the opposite state.","Welcome concerns another sense.","Opening does not express the closed state."],"Closed opposes open in this setting.")]),prereq=[16,24])

unit(34,"Compound nouns and collocation","recognise a compound noun, choose a conventional form and preserve the relevant head in a plural",
 "word structure, nouns and natural combinations","later formal writing and dictionary-based vocabulary extension",
 "A compound noun combines elements into a naming expression. It may be written as one word, with a hyphen or as separate words. The spelling is conventional, so check a learner’s dictionary rather than imposing one style on every compound. Many pluralise the last noun, but some mark a different head: mothers-in-law is a familiar example. Collocation concerns conventional combinations more broadly. A phrase can be natural without every combination being a compound noun.",
 "Original examples: notebook, bus stop and mother-in-law use different written styles. Their plurals need attention to the naming unit, not a rule that adds -s to the final letter of every expression. A learner takes notes and makes a decision; exchanging every verb with a synonym may spoil the collocation.",
 "Identify what the compound names and consult its established spelling. Find the head when forming a plural. Keep spelling, word class and meaning distinct; a space does not stop an expression functioning as a compound noun. The lesson checks written recognition, not spoken stress.",[
 F("closed compound","a naming compound written as one word in the model","Notebook is written together in this model.","Every compound noun must contain a space.","Compounds have several conventional written styles."),F("open compound","a naming compound written as separate words in the model","Bus stop functions as a compound despite the space.","A space proves an expression cannot be a compound noun.","The naming expression can contain separate written words."),F("head-based plural","a plural marking the appropriate central noun in the compound","Mother-in-law becomes mothers-in-law.","Mother-in-law must always become mother-in-laws.","The head mother takes the plural in this model."),F("collocational choice","the conventional combination needed for a natural phrase","The learner makes a decision and takes notes.","Any familiar verb can replace make in make a decision naturally.","The whole combination must be checked.")],
 choose(34,[("Choose the conventional plural in the model.","mothers-in-law",["mother-in-laws","mothers-ins-law","mother-in-lawes"],["The plural belongs on mother.","Do not pluralise the preposition.","That ending does not form the standard plural."],"Mother is the head in this compound."),
 ("Which expression can be an open compound noun?","bus stop",["very quietly","has written","although it rains"],["This is an adverbial expression.","This is a verb phrase.","This is a dependent clause."],"Bus stop names one kind of place despite its space."),
 ("Choose the natural model collocation.","take notes",["do notes, as the standard model","heavy notes, meaning write them","notes take, as the ordinary verb-object order"],["Take is the conventional verb here.","That does not give the requested writing action.","The verb-object order has been reversed."],"Take notes is a conventional combination."),
 ("Which rule is accurate?","Check a compound’s conventional spelling.",["All compounds must be hyphenated.","All compounds must be closed words.","No plural compound can contain a space."],["Some are open or closed.","Some are hyphenated or open.","Open compounds can have plurals."],"Spelling varies by the established compound.")]),prereq=[16,31])

writing(5,"Introduction to directed writing","produce a response that fulfils the specified purpose, audience, form and content points",
 "sentence accuracy and familiar school communication","essay planning, letters and later talks or reports",
 "Directed writing provides a situation and specific instructions. Identify your role, audience, purpose and required form before drafting. Underline every content point and decide where it belongs. A fluent response can still fail its purpose if it omits an essential instruction or uses the wrong format. Use a suitable tone and distinguish information given by the task from assumptions. Check the completed text against the directions rather than marking it by length alone.",
 "CLASSROOM MODEL NOTICE. Reading Club Meeting. Members are invited to a planning meeting in the school library on Monday afternoon. We will choose a shared reading activity and agree how to care for borrowed books. Please bring a suggestion and a notebook. Issued by the fictional club secretary. The model gives audience, place, time and purpose without claiming an actual school event.",
 "Make a short checklist from the prompt. Plan the order, draft the required form and check each point. Add only suitable invented details where the task permits them. Do not replace a requested notice with an unrelated essay simply because its sentences are correct.",[
 F("purpose","the result the writing is intended to achieve","The notice invites members to a planning meeting.","A response fulfils every task merely by containing correct spelling.","Correct language must also serve the requested purpose."),F("audience","the intended readers of the response","The notice addresses reading-club members.","Audience can never affect tone or what must be explained.","Readers’ needs help determine the language and details."),F("required form","the kind of text specified in the instructions","The instruction asks for a notice rather than an essay.","A notice and a private letter always have the same layout.","The form affects layout and conventions."),F("content checklist","a record of the points the prompt requires","The writer checks that place, time and purpose are included.","A writer should ignore the required details after choosing a title.","A final checklist catches omissions.")],
 "You are secretary of a fictional school reading club. Write a notice inviting members to a planning meeting. Give the audience, place, time, purpose and something members should bring. Use no private contact details.",
 ["My notice fulfils the stated role, audience and purpose.","Every required content point appears.","My tone, layout, sentences and spelling suit a notice."],prereq=[3,4])

writing(8,"Essay planning, thesis statements and topic sentences","plan a focused essay with a controlling idea and paragraphs that develop it",
 "directed-writing purpose and sentence accuracy","argumentative and narrative writing with an organised structure",
 "An essay needs a focus and an order that helps the reader. A thesis statement presents the controlling idea or position; a topic sentence gives the main idea of a paragraph. Supporting details explain, illustrate or qualify that idea. A plan is an outline, not the final essay. An introduction should prepare the reader for the focus, while a conclusion draws together the developed ideas. Avoid claiming that every essay type needs an identical rigid formula.",
 "Original plan on reading clubs: Focus: a club can support reading when members participate. Paragraph idea: shared discussion clarifies understanding. Support: members compare reasons for an interpretation. Paragraph idea: organisation makes participation possible. Support: clear roles and suitable meeting times. Ending: the club’s value depends on active use. A sample topic sentence is Shared discussion helps members test their understanding.",
 "State one manageable focus. Group related points and give each paragraph a clear role. Check that evidence supports the paragraph idea and that the paragraph serves the overall focus. Do not confuse a paragraph’s opening sentence with the whole essay’s thesis.",[
 F("thesis statement","the essay’s controlling idea or position","The planned essay argues that participation makes a reading club useful.","A thesis must list every possible unrelated topic.","A controlling idea gives the essay focus."),F("topic sentence","the main idea guiding a paragraph","Shared discussion helps members test their understanding introduces one paragraph.","A topic sentence must always be the entire essay’s conclusion.","It guides the paragraph’s development."),F("supporting detail","an explanation or example developing a paragraph’s idea","Members compare reasons for their interpretations illustrates useful discussion.","Any unrelated anecdote automatically supports a paragraph.","Support must connect to the specific idea."),F("plan","an outline arranging ideas before drafting","The writer groups discussion and organisation in separate paragraph ideas.","An outline is automatically a finished essay.","The plan guides later development into connected prose.")],
 "Plan an essay on how a school club can help learners. Write a controlling statement, paragraph ideas with support and a proposed conclusion. Develop one planned paragraph into prose.",
 ["My focus is clear and manageable.","Each planned paragraph has relevant support.","My developed paragraph follows its topic sentence."],prereq=[5])

writing(13,"Formal and informal letters","write a letter whose layout, tone and content fit its reader and purpose",
 "directed-writing instructions and paragraph planning","talks, reports and controlled changes of register",
 "A letter’s relationship and purpose affect its tone. A formal school request uses respectful clear wording, a suitable salutation, organised content and an appropriate close. An informal letter to a friend can sound more personal while remaining clear. Include the addresses, date and other elements the task and examination convention require; exact layouts may vary. Do not treat contractions as universally forbidden or friendliness as an excuse for missing the purpose. Use fictional addresses in classroom practice.",
 "Formal miniature: Dear Sir or Madam, I am writing on behalf of our fictional reading club to request permission to use the library after lessons. We propose supervised discussion and careful return of materials. Please let us know the arrangements required. Yours faithfully, Bih. Informal miniature: Dear Ayuk, Our club is planning a reading afternoon. I hope you can join us and bring a story suggestion. Write and tell me what you think. Best wishes, Bih.",
 "Plan the request or message before adding the conventional frame. Match salutation and close, give the reason and state the response wanted. For the paper task, add fictitious address/date details in the teacher’s preferred layout. Compare tone without inventing one universal layout for every context.",[
 F("formal register","language suited to an official or less familiar relationship","The writer politely requests permission from an institution.","Formal means using insults and unnecessarily difficult words.","Clarity and respect support a formal request."),F("informal register","language suited to a familiar personal relationship","Bih writes a friendly invitation to Ayuk.","Informal writing has no need for a clear message.","A personal tone still needs understandable purpose and content."),F("salutation and close","the greeting and ending chosen for the letter’s relationship and convention","The model pairs Dear Sir or Madam with Yours faithfully.","Any greeting automatically fits every closing and audience.","Match the frame to the intended relationship and convention."),F("requested response","what the letter asks the reader to do or answer","The formal writer asks about the arrangements required.","A request letter should never indicate what answer or action it seeks.","The reader needs a clear requested action.")],
 "Using wholly fictional address/date details, write a formal letter requesting use of a school room, then a short informal letter inviting a friend to the planned activity. Give reasons and the response wanted.",
 ["Each letter fits its audience and purpose.","I use a suitable frame and organised paragraphs.","I check all task points, grammar and fictional personal details."],prereq=[5,8,12])

writing(22,"Argumentative essays","develop a position with reasons, relevant support and a fair response to a counterargument",
 "essay focus, contextual evidence and conditional reasoning","stronger paragraph structure and balanced formal argument",
 "An argumentative essay defends a position through reasons. A claim needs support that bears on it; repeating a preference is not evidence. A counterargument presents a relevant objection, and a response explains how the writer addresses it. A conclusion should follow the discussion rather than introduce an unsupported new case. An argument can be firm without insulting opponents. Do not invent statistics, expert quotations or universal outcomes merely to make a paragraph sound persuasive.",
 "Original model: A school reading club can be useful when it provides structured discussion. Members can compare interpretations and explain their reasons rather than merely exchange answers. Some may object that meetings take time from individual study. This concern matters, so a club should use suitable meeting times and focused tasks. Participation should be purposeful, not compulsory busywork. A well-organised club can therefore complement individual reading, although its value depends on how members use it.",
 "Choose a clear position and outline reasons with examples. Present an objection accurately before answering it. Qualify a claim when the evidence has limits. Check whether the conclusion is supported and whether the reader can distinguish an invented illustration from a verified statistic.",[
 F("position","the main view the writer defends","The model supports a purposeful reading club.","An argument must change to an unrelated position in every paragraph.","A clear position organises the reasoning."),F("relevant support","a reason or example bearing on the stated claim","Comparing interpretations illustrates a learning benefit.","Any impressive number counts as evidence even if invented.","Support must be relevant and honestly presented."),F("counterargument","a relevant objection to the writer’s view","The model recognises the time that meetings use.","A counterargument must be an insult to the writer.","An objection can address costs or reasoning without attacking a person."),F("reasoned response","an answer explaining how an objection is addressed or limits the view","Focused tasks and suitable timing answer part of the time concern.","Ignoring an objection automatically refutes it.","A response needs an explanation, not silence or repetition.")],
 "Write an argumentative essay on whether a school club can complement individual study. State a view, develop reasons, address a fair objection and reach a supported conclusion. Label invented examples and do not fabricate statistics.",
 ["My position and reasons are clear.","My support is relevant and I address an objection fairly.","My conclusion follows my reasoning and my language remains respectful."],prereq=[8,16,17,21])

writing(25,"Narrative essays","write a coherent original narrative with setting, linked events and a meaningful ending",
 "paragraph planning, sentence structure and simple-past events","past-perfect chronology and later report/narrative contrasts",
 "A narrative tells linked events from a chosen viewpoint. Establish the situation, introduce a change or difficulty, develop actions and provide an ending that relates to them. Time signals, pronoun reference and verb choices help the reader follow the sequence. Description should support the events, not interrupt them with an unrelated catalogue. A narrative may be invented; pupils need not disclose private incidents. A dramatic story still needs consistency in what characters know and do.",
 "Original model: Bih reached the fictional club room and found the notice missing. Members were gathering outside, unsure whether the meeting would happen. She checked with the teacher instead of announcing a cancellation. The room was ready; the notice had fallen behind a shelf. Bih replaced it and helped members enter. The small misunderstanding showed her why checking a source mattered more than spreading a quick guess.",
 "Plan the setting, difficulty, actions and outcome. Choose a viewpoint and preserve it. Make transitions clear and reread each pronoun. Invent suitable details if you prefer, but do not change an object’s location or a character’s knowledge accidentally just to force the ending.",[
 F("setting","the place, time and circumstances establishing a narrative","The model begins at a fictional club room before a meeting.","A narrative must give no clues about where events occur.","Relevant context helps a reader follow the events."),F("complication","a change or difficulty setting actions in motion","The missing notice creates uncertainty.","A complication never affects the actions that follow.","The difficulty should motivate relevant responses."),F("causal sequence","events linked by understandable reasons and consequences","Bih checks with a teacher because the notice is missing.","A list of unrelated events automatically forms a coherent plot.","Actions should relate logically as well as occur in time."),F("resolution","the outcome addressing the narrative’s difficulty","The notice is found and the meeting proceeds.","A resolution should introduce an unrelated essay topic.","The ending relates to the difficulty developed.")],
 "Write an original narrative about a misunderstanding that is resolved by checking information. Use an imaginary character and setting, linked actions and a coherent ending.",
 ["My reader can follow the setting and sequence.","My viewpoint, pronouns and tenses remain coherent.","My ending grows from the problem and actions."],prereq=[8,23])

writing(28,"Writing a talk for an audience","prepare a talk with an appropriate address, clear message, supporting points and a purposeful ending",
 "directed writing, letters and argumentative paragraph organisation","spoken delivery practice and later reports for different audiences",
 "A written talk is meant to be heard by a particular audience. A greeting can establish the relationship; a clear opening gives the topic and purpose. Develop a few connected points using language listeners can follow. Signposts such as first and finally can help without replacing actual reasoning. A conclusion draws the message together and may propose a suitable action. This task prepares the script; a text-only screen cannot assess pace, voice, eye contact or listening response.",
 "Original model: Fellow learners, our reading room belongs to everyone who uses it. Today I ask us to keep its materials useful. First, return each book to the agreed place so another reader can find it. Next, report damaged materials rather than hiding the problem. Finally, listen when others explain the borrowing arrangements. Small organised actions can make shared reading easier. Let us agree on responsibilities and review how well they work. Thank you.",
 "State audience, occasion and purpose before drafting. Use examples and direct language suited to listeners. Read the script with a partner or teacher if possible, but do not confuse a completed written task with an assessed oral performance. Preserve factual accuracy even when seeking a memorable phrase.",[
 F("audience address","the opening acknowledgement suited to the listeners","Fellow learners addresses the school audience.","Every talk must begin with a private bank account number.","The opening should establish a suitable audience relationship."),F("signposting","wording that guides listeners through the ideas","First and finally indicate stages in the talk.","Signposts alone provide all the evidence a talk needs.","They organise content but do not replace support."),F("supporting example","a concrete case making the message understandable","Returning a book explains how shared access can improve.","An example must be unrelated to the main message.","A relevant example helps listeners follow the point."),F("purposeful ending","a close that draws together the message or suitable action","The talk invites agreement on responsibilities.","A talk should end by changing to a wholly unrelated subject.","The ending should follow the developed message.")],
 "Write a talk for fellow learners about caring for a shared school resource. Give a suitable address, connected supported points and a purposeful ending. Oral delivery can be practised separately with a teacher.",
 ["My opening fits the audience and purpose.","My points and examples are easy for a listener to follow.","My ending follows the message; I have checked accuracy and grammar."],prereq=[5,13,22])

writing(32,"Writing a factual report","organise findings and recommendations while separating observation, inference and proposal",
 "narrative chronology, audience and formal-purpose writing","later evidence-based summaries and reports",
 "A report presents information for a stated reader and purpose. A heading, introduction, organised findings and a conclusion or recommendations can help. Exact format depends on the task. Separate what was observed from an inference and from what should happen next. Use clear factual wording and appropriate tense. A fictional classroom report must be labelled as such; it should not claim that a real school event or inspection occurred. A report differs from a story seeking suspense or an essay primarily defending a personal preference.",
 "FICTIONAL CLASSROOM REPORT: Reading-room materials. Purpose: to review the model room’s organisation. Findings: some books were placed away from their labelled shelves, and the borrowing notice was not easily visible. The record does not establish who moved the books. Recommendation: place the notice where readers can see it and agree how returned materials are sorted. A later check should establish whether the arrangement works. Prepared by an imaginary club secretary.",
 "Identify the reader, purpose and permitted information. Group findings logically and link each recommendation to a finding. Do not invent a responsible person when the evidence does not identify one. Check headings, sentence accuracy and whether an inference is being presented as if directly observed.",[
 F("finding","information established by the stated observation or record","The fictional record notes books away from labelled shelves.","A finding must always name a culprit even when none is known.","Report only what the record supports."),F("inference","an interpretation drawn from evidence rather than directly observed","A writer suggests a possible organisational cause but marks it as a possibility.","An inference is automatically a directly witnessed event.","Distinguish an interpretation from an observation."),F("recommendation","a proposed action responding to the findings","The model proposes making the borrowing notice easier to see.","A recommendation proves the proposed action has already happened.","It proposes a future response, not an accomplished event."),F("report purpose","the information need that guides the report’s selection and organisation","The model reviews organisation for a fictional club reader.","A report must ignore its intended reader and question.","Purpose shapes relevant findings and presentation.")],
 "Write a clearly labelled fictional report on the organisation of a school resource. Give purpose, findings and linked recommendations. Separate known facts, uncertain interpretations and proposed actions; do not accuse a real person.",
 ["My headings and order suit the purpose.","I distinguish findings, inferences and recommendations.","Each recommendation relates to evidence; I invent no culprit."],prereq=[23,25,28,29,31])

# Speaking rows: preparation, respectful discussion and short original exchanges.
SPEAKING=[
 (2,"Discussing care for family members and friends","offer and discuss respectful practical support without making assumptions about a person’s needs",
  "Form Three’s polite requests and reasoned suggestions","family-life discussion and directed writing for an audience",
  "A discussion of care should ask what support a person wants instead of assuming every older person, child or disabled person has identical needs. Use polite offers, relevant questions and clear reasons. An offer is not permission to ignore a refusal. Language practice uses fictional cases; pupils do not need to disclose private health or family information. It does not train a learner to administer treatment or take over an adult’s medical responsibilities.",
  "Original dialogue: Bih asks an imaginary older neighbour, Would you like help with this light bag? The neighbour accepts and says where it should go. Ayuk asks a friend what would make a group activity easier to join. If a younger sibling seems unwell, the fictional learner tells a responsible adult instead of inventing treatment.",
  [F("polite offer","a courteous proposal of assistance that leaves a choice","Would you like help with this light bag?","An offer means ignoring every refusal.","The person should be able to accept or decline."),F("clarifying question","a question establishing what support is actually needed","What would make the activity easier for you to join?","All support needs can be inferred from appearance alone.","Ask about the particular need rather than assuming it."),F("respectful language","wording that preserves a person’s dignity","A speaker discusses relevant support without insulting labels.","Respect requires avoiding any practical question.","A relevant question can be considerate and clear."),F("appropriate referral","seeking a responsible adult or trained support for a concern beyond the learner’s role","A learner tells a responsible adult that a sibling seems unwell.","A dialogue authorises a child to prescribe treatment.","Language practice does not supply clinical training or authority.")]),
 (9,"Discussing aspects of family life","exchange views about shared responsibilities, respect and communication using fictional situations",
  "polite offers, directed purpose and careful word choice","reading an original family-life passage and writing to different audiences",
  "Family-life discussions can examine cooperation, communication and differing responsibilities. Avoid claiming that every household has the same members or arrangements. Give reasons, listen to another view and distinguish a suggestion from a command. Learners can use fictional households and are not required to share private events. A disagreement should address the action or proposal rather than a person’s worth. Respectful language can still express a clear limit or seek help.",
  "Original dialogue: In a fictional household, study time overlaps with chores. Bih proposes discussing the fixed deadlines. Ayuk says a fair plan must consider each person’s responsibilities and available time. They agree to ask a responsible adult to help adjust the arrangement. The exchange offers a possible process, not a judgment that every household must follow one schedule.",
  [F("shared responsibility","work or duties considered together within the stated arrangement","The household discusses how chores and study fit together.","Fairness always means identical tasks for every person regardless of circumstances.","Responsibilities should be considered with the actual circumstances."),F("reasoned suggestion","a proposed action supported by an explanation","Discuss fixed deadlines because the activities overlap.","A suggestion needs no connection to the stated difficulty.","A useful proposal responds to the actual problem."),F("active listening","attending to another person’s view before responding","Ayuk restates the concern before proposing an adjustment.","Listening means preparing an insult before the other person finishes.","Understand the point before answering it."),F("respectful boundary","a clear limit expressed without degrading another person","A speaker declines to share private household details.","Class participation requires private family disclosure.","Fictional examples allow participation while respecting privacy.")]),
 (14,"Discussing club and association activities","express a club preference with reasons, ask for clarification and propose a shared activity",
  "family-life discussion, adverbs and letter purpose","future plans and argumentative writing about school participation",
  "A useful club discussion combines an opinion with a reason and an example. Preferences do not establish universal facts. Ask a precise question when an activity is unclear, and acknowledge another view before responding. A proposal suggests what could happen; it does not report an event already completed. This is written preparation for speaking. A partner or teacher can later assess actual turn-taking, voice and listening, which the screen does not hear.",
  "Original dialogue: Bih prefers the reading club because members compare stories. Ayuk prefers the environmental club because he enjoys organising practical projects. Bih asks whether the groups could share an exhibition. Ayuk suggests checking space and supervision first. Their preferences remain different, while the proposal creates a possible shared task.",
  [F("opinion","a stated personal view or preference","Bih prefers the reading club.","One preference proves every learner wants the same activity.","A preference describes the speaker’s view, not a universal fact."),F("reason","an explanation supporting the stated view","Bih enjoys comparing stories, so the club interests her.","Any unrelated statement supports the view equally.","The reason must bear on the preference."),F("clarification","a question seeking a more exact explanation","Could you explain what the proposed exhibition would include?","Clarification means changing to an unrelated topic.","It makes the present proposal clearer."),F("proposal","an action suggested for consideration","Could the groups share an exhibition?","A proposal proves the event already happened.","It suggests a possible action, not a completed event.")]),
 (30,"Discussing environmental protection","propose a realistic environmental action, explain its purpose and recognise a limitation",
  "reasoned discussion, future expressions and talks for an audience","word-building, reports and evidence-based hygiene/safety reading",
  "An environmental discussion should connect a specific action to its purpose. Reducing waste prevents some waste at source; reusing gives an appropriate object another use; recycling requires a suitable treatment route. Sorting alone does not prove a material is recycled. Use the school’s actual instructions for practical work and keep unknown or dangerous waste outside pupils’ handling tasks. This lesson practises language through fictional proposals rather than arranging an unsupervised activity.",
  "Original dialogue: Bih proposes avoiding unnecessary printing. Ayuk suggests reusing a clean box for materials. Another member asks which materials the local collection arrangement accepts. The group will check the teacher’s instructions before acting. Each proposal explains a possible benefit without claiming that one small action solves every environmental problem.",
  [F("reduction","avoiding part of the resource use or waste at source","The group proposes fewer unnecessary printed sheets.","Reduction necessarily means producing more waste.","The aim is to prevent part of the use or waste."),F("reuse","giving an appropriate object another use","A clean box is used again for storage.","Every reuse requires industrial material transformation.","The object can remain intact while its use changes."),F("recycling","transforming a material through a suitable treatment route","The group asks whether an accepted material has a real recycling route.","Sorting any object guarantees it has already been recycled.","Collection, sorting and actual processing are different stages."),F("qualified proposal","a suggested action recognising relevant conditions or limits","Members check accepted materials and teacher instructions before acting.","A good proposal must claim it solves every problem instantly.","Specific conditions make a proposal more realistic.")])]

for n,title,goal,back,next_use,note,model,facts in SPEAKING:
    unit(n,title,goal,back,next_use,note,model,
      "Prepare short turns: state the point, give a relevant reason and invite a response. Use fictional people if the subject is private. Practise with a partner when available; written choices here do not establish oral fluency or listening ability. Challenge an idea without insulting the speaker.",facts,
      word_order(n,["Could you explain the proposal, please?","I understand your point, but I have a question.","This suggestion needs a clear reason.","Let us check the conditions before deciding."]),prereq={2:[],9:[2,5],14:[9,12,13],30:[14,15,28]}[n])

# Pronunciation rows: text/symbol preparation, explicitly separate from hearing or speech assessment.
for n,title,low,high,left,right in [(7,"Written preparation: /θ/ and /ð/","/θ/","/ð/",["think","thin","thank","thing"],["this","that","these","those"]),
 (19,"Written preparation: /tʃ/ and /dʒ/","/tʃ/","/dʒ/",["chair","cheap","chalk","choose"],["judge","jam","joy","join"])]:
    select=choose(n,[("Which written model begins with "+low+"?",w,right[:3],[x+" begins with "+high+" in this model." for x in right[:3]],"Compare the supplied model words and their initial consonants.") for w in left])
    sound=choose(n,[("Identify the initial consonant in the written pronunciation model for "+w+".",s,[o for o in [low,high,"/s/","/z/"] if o!=s],
      [("This reverses the model’s voicing: "+o+" is "+("voiceless" if o==low else "voiced")+", while "+s+" is "+("voiceless" if s==low else "voiced")+".") if o in [low,high] else
       (o+" is an alveolar fricative; the model for "+w+" uses "+("a dental fricative" if n==7 else "an affricate, with a stop followed by friction")+".") for o in [low,high,"/s/","/z/"] if o!=s],"The model gives "+w+" an initial "+s+".") for words,s in [(left,low),(right,high)] for w in words],key="sound")
    unit(n,title,"group written model words by their initial consonant and explain the stated voicing distinction",
      "written sound-symbol preparation and careful dictionary use","clearer word recognition and later teacher-led pronunciation practice",
      "Phonetic symbols represent sounds, not ordinary letter names. The pair contrasts a voiceless model with a voiced model. Voicing concerns vocal-fold vibration, not capitals or loudness. "+("Both are dental fricatives: the tongue approaches the teeth and air passes with friction." if n==7 else "Both are affricates: a brief closure releases into friction. They are not the plain /s/ and /z/ fricatives, made near the ridge behind the upper teeth.")+" The screen supplies written models only. Use a teacher or reliable recording to practise the actual sounds; printed choices cannot assess your pronunciation.",
      "Written model groups: "+", ".join(left)+" begin with "+low+"; "+", ".join(right)+" begin with "+high+". Compare the initial consonant, not the word’s final letter. Different spellings can sometimes represent the same sound, so spelling alone is not a universal pronunciation rule. These are standard model examples to verify with the teacher’s pronunciation convention.",
      "Identify which sound the model supplies, then group the written examples. For the actual contrast, listen to a model and practise separately with a teacher. Do not interpret a correct multiple-choice answer as proof that you have heard or produced the sound accurately. Word-initial examples avoid claiming that every occurrence of a letter combination behaves identically.",[
      F("voiceless model "+low,"the initial consonant group represented by "+low+" in the examples",left[0]+" begins with "+low+" in the written model.","Every word in this lesson begins with the voiced consonant.","The models separate the voiceless and voiced groups."),
      F("voiced model "+high,"the initial consonant group represented by "+high+" in the examples",right[0]+" begins with "+high+" in the written model.","Voiced means only that a word is written in capitals.","Voicing concerns vocal-fold vibration, not letter case."),
      F("sound-symbol distinction","the difference between phonetic notation and ordinary spelling","The symbols label model sounds rather than spelling letters.","A single spelling rule predicts every unfamiliar word’s pronunciation.","Check pronunciation rather than generalising the spelling without limits."),
      F("written preparation","recognition of models before a separate audio or spoken activity","The learner groups printed examples before practising with a teacher.","The silent screen proves that a learner pronounced every consonant correctly.","Text recognition and actual speech/listening assessment are different.")],select,prereq={7:[3,6],19:[7,16]}[n],extra=sound)

# Original reading/listening-preparation passages; every comprehension answer has a textual warrant.
def reading(n,title,goal,back,next_use,passage,rows,prereq=(),oral=False):
    facts=[F("explicit detail","information directly stated in the passage","The reader repeats a place or action the passage actually states.","An explicit detail is always something invented by the reader.","Direct information appears in the passage."),
      F("supported inference","an interpretation linked to clues in the passage","The reader explains a possible intention using described actions.","Any private guess is a proven inference.","An inference needs textual support."),
      F("main idea","the central message organising the passage","A summary brings together the important actions without unrelated additions.","A random minor word always gives the complete main idea.","The main idea must represent the relevant whole."),
      F("information not supplied","a detail the passage does not allow the reader to determine","The reader refuses to invent a price or result that is absent.","An absent detail must always be replaced with a guessed number.","A faithful answer acknowledges missing information.")]
    question=choose(n,[(passage+"\n"+q,r,w,e,why) for q,r,w,e,why in rows])
    second=choose(n,[("Check this reading claim about the original passage: "+claim,answer,w,e,why) for claim,answer,w,e,why in [
      ("A missing price or name can be guessed and reported as stated fact.","No: report only what the passage supplies or supports.",["Yes: every guess is an explicit detail.","Yes: a familiar name must be the writer’s chosen name.","Yes: a price from another story must apply."],["A guess is not direct information.","Familiarity does not provide textual evidence.","A different story supplies no fact about this passage."],"Missing information stays unestablished."),
      ("A summary should preserve the central actions rather than add an unrelated event.","Yes: keep the summary faithful to the passage.",["No: summaries must invent a new ending.","No: any unrelated opinion replaces the main idea.","No: a summary must be longer than every source text."],["A summary does not invent a resolution.","An unrelated opinion is not a faithful summary.","Length alone is not the criterion."],"Select and connect the important points without invention."),
      ("A proposed action proves that the action has already been completed.","No: a proposal and a completed event are different.",["Yes: every suggestion is a reported result.","Yes: future wording is always past evidence.","Yes: planning proves every intended outcome."],["A suggestion is not a result.","Tense and meaning matter.","A plan can fail or change."],"Preserve the status and time of the action."),
      ("An inference should be linked to a clue, rather than just an interesting possibility.","Yes: explain which textual detail supports it.",["No: evidence is irrelevant.","No: all possible events are proven.","No: a confident voice establishes every claim."],["Evidence matters to the inference.","Possible is not the same as established.","Confidence does not replace textual support."],"A defensible inference uses evidence.")]],key="evidence")
    unit(n,title,goal,back,next_use,
      ("This is written preparation for a listening lesson. Read the original script for meaning, key information and unknown words. It provides no audio and cannot assess listening comprehension. A teacher or recording should later supply an oral version; the questions here assess the written script. " if oral else "Reading involves following what a passage states, connecting its ideas and distinguishing supported inference from invention. ")+
      "Identify the main idea and locate evidence for each answer. A question may concern a detail, reason, sequence or word in context. If the text omits information, do not supply an invented fact. A plausible outcome is not necessarily a reported result.",
      passage,"Read the whole passage before answering. Point to a phrase supporting the answer and explain any inference. When summarising, preserve who did what and whether an action was planned or completed. These original classroom texts are not extracts from prescribed literature.",facts,question,prereq=prereq,extra=second)

reading(6,"Written preparation: studying-skills listening script","extract the main idea and supporting details from an original study-skills script",
 "directed writing and organising task points","later listening practice and evidence-based reading",
 "Original script: Bih recognises definitions while reading but cannot explain them later. Ayuk suggests closing the book, recalling one idea and then checking it. They agree to alternate questions after household tasks. Bih says the first attempt may show gaps; correcting those gaps matters more than copying an answer. Their plan does not promise instant success or give a fixed study timetable for every pupil.",[
 ("What difficulty does Bih describe?","Recognising definitions but failing to explain them later.",["Being unable to open any book.","Having no household tasks.","Winning a prize for every answer."],["The script describes recall difficulty, not opening books.","Household tasks are mentioned.","No prize is reported."],"The opening states her difficulty."),
 ("What action does Ayuk suggest before checking?","Close the book and recall an idea.",["Copy the answer while looking at it.","Never check any attempt.","Destroy the notes."],["Copying is contrasted with recall.","Checking is part of the suggestion.","This action is absent."],"The script describes recall before checking."),
 ("What is the value of finding a gap?","It identifies something to correct.",["It proves the pupil can never learn.","It guarantees a prize.","It means every source is false."],["The script treats gaps as improvable.","No prize guarantee appears.","A recall gap does not disprove the source."],"Correction, not copying, is emphasised."),
 ("Which claim is not supported?","The plan guarantees immediate success for every pupil.",["The learners alternate questions.","They work after household tasks.","They check recalled ideas."],["That action is stated.","That timing is stated.","Checking is stated."],"The script expressly avoids a success guarantee.")],prereq=[5],oral=True)

reading(10,"Reading an original family-life passage","identify a problem, proposed response and limits of the evidence in a family-life passage",
 "respectful discussion and shared-responsibility language","letters, argument and clear reports of information",
 "Original passage: In an imaginary household in Bamenda, Bih’s revision time overlaps with a younger sibling’s reading practice. She asks a responsible adult to help discuss the schedule. The family lists fixed tasks and available time before proposing an adjustment. The passage does not say that every household has the same arrangement or that the new plan has already solved every difficulty.",[
 ("What is the stated difficulty?","Revision time overlaps with a sibling’s reading practice.",["The household has no books.","The club is closed forever.","A journey has already failed."],["No absence of books is stated.","No club closure is described.","There is no journey in this passage."],"The first sentence identifies the overlap."),
 ("Who does Bih ask for help?","A responsible adult.",["A named bank manager.","An unknown online seller.","A particular medical specialist."],["No bank manager is named.","No seller appears.","No medical referral is described."],"The passage states the kind of support requested."),
 ("Why list fixed tasks and available time?","To inform a realistic schedule adjustment.",["To prove everyone has identical duties.","To guarantee every problem is solved.","To decide a real medical diagnosis."],["The text does not impose identical duties.","A proposed plan is not a guarantee.","This is a scheduling discussion."],"The listed constraints are relevant to an adjustment."),
 ("Which result is established?","An adjustment is proposed, but its final success is not reported.",["Every difficulty has permanently disappeared.","The family has moved to another country.","All households adopted this exact plan."],["The text denies that this outcome is established.","No move is reported.","One imaginary household does not establish a universal result."],"The final sentence limits what can be concluded.")],prereq=[2,9])

reading(20,"Reading: e-commerce and face-to-face trade","compare the passage’s trading arrangements and interpret its vocabulary without assuming unreported prices or outcomes",
 "context clues, collocations and reasoned comparison","argumentative writing and checking claims about transactions",
 "Original passage: An imaginary Douala seller displays goods online and at a stall. An online customer asks about the item, total price and delivery before deciding. A stall customer can inspect the displayed item but still needs clear information. The seller keeps a transaction record. Online ordering can extend contact beyond the stall, yet a listing alone proves neither product quality nor guaranteed delivery. No actual price or platform is named.",[
 ("What does the online customer ask about?","The item, total price and delivery.",["A classmate’s private password.","A guaranteed investment profit.","Only the seller’s favourite colour."],["No password request is described.","No investment is involved.","The text gives transaction questions."],"The passage lists these questions."),
 ("What advantage is described for the stall customer?","Inspecting the displayed item.",["Knowing every online seller’s honesty.","Avoiding any need for information.","Receiving a guaranteed free delivery."],["That conclusion is unsupported.","The passage says clear information remains needed.","No such delivery is offered."],"Inspection is the stated difference."),
 ("What is a transaction record in this context?","A written account of the exchange.",["A guarantee that no problem can happen.","A customer’s secret password.","An unrelated music recording."],["A record is not an absolute guarantee.","The passage does not request a password.","The business context determines the meaning."],"The trading context supports a record of the exchange."),
 ("Which information cannot be determined?","The actual price charged.",["Whether the seller uses a stall.","Whether delivery is discussed.","Whether a record is kept."],["The stall is explicitly stated.","Delivery is explicitly mentioned.","The record is explicitly mentioned."],"No actual price is named.")],prereq=[16,18])

reading(33,"Reading: hygiene and safety","use a passage to distinguish an authorised plan, a precaution and a completed result",
 "environmental discussion and factual report writing","careful use of instructions and later health/safety vocabulary",
 "Original passage: A fictional school club plans to improve access to handwashing. Members check that water and soap are available and ask the teacher which tasks pupils may do. They propose a clear sign near the facility. They do not handle unknown chemicals or sharp waste. The passage reports preparation; it does not claim that a new facility is complete or that handwashing prevents every possible illness.",[
 ("What materials does the club check?","Water and soap.",["Unknown chemicals for pupils to mix.","A medical prescription.","A shop’s banking details."],["The passage excludes handling unknown chemicals.","No prescription appears.","No banking details are requested."],"The passage names water and soap."),
 ("Which action is a precaution?","Avoiding unknown chemicals and sharp waste.",["Handling every sharp item to practise.","Mixing unidentified products.","Ignoring the teacher’s task limits."],["That contradicts the precaution.","That contradicts the chemical limit.","The teacher’s authorisation is checked."],"The stated limit avoids unauthorised hazardous handling."),
 ("Why ask the teacher about tasks?","To establish what pupils are authorised to do.",["To prove the facility is already complete.","To obtain a guaranteed cure.","To replace all classroom learning with a rumour."],["Authorisation is not a completed construction.","The text supplies no cure.","This is not the described purpose."],"The question concerns authorised participation."),
 ("Which summary is faithful?","The club prepares a supervised improvement and recognises safety limits.",["All illnesses have ended.","The new facility is already complete.","The club secretly tests chemicals."],["The passage rejects a universal illness guarantee.","The work is a plan.","The passage excludes unknown chemical handling."],"Keep the distinction between preparation and result.")],prereq=[30,32])

CURRENCIES=[("Cameroon","Central African CFA franc (XAF)"),("Nigeria","naira (NGN)"),("Ghana","Ghana cedi (GHS)"),("United Kingdom","pound sterling (GBP)")]
amountq=num("e4-26-amount",2,"Write this classroom amount in figures: {words(amount)} CFA francs.","amount",["Read the complete number phrase and its place values.","The amount is {answer} CFA francs."],vars={"a":{"int":[11,99]},"b":{"int":[1,9]},"amount":{"expr":"a*1000+b*100"}},misc=[("tenfold","amount*10","An extra place-value zero makes this ten times too large."),("short","amount/10","Removing a place-value zero makes this ten times too small.")],unit="CFA francs",sources=SRC)
